<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    nexus-gacz0 (review round): index the read path of GET /v1/catalog/by_title
    (CatalogRepository.documentsByTitle), an exact title equality over a tenant's live
    rows. Without an index the query is a sequential scan of the tenant's catalog_documents.

    MEASURED (throwaway Testcontainers run, PostgreSQL 17.11, database collation en_US.utf8,
    tenant of 250,000 documents, 95 percent live, beside a second tenant a tenth the size,
    12 live documents carrying the searched title across six owners):
      25,000 documents    sequential scan   3 to 4 ms   (no index to compare; 44 ms build, 1.5 MB)
      250,000 documents   parallel seq scan 22 to 35 ms, 10.9k buffers
                          with this index   0.02 to 0.05 ms, 4 to 6 buffers
                          index build       361 ms, 15 MB
    With an owner prefix the plan was the same scan: tumbler is plain TEXT and the
    database collation is a locale one, so a LIKE 'p.%' prefix cannot become a range on the
    (tenant_id, tumbler) primary key and stays a filter. The owner prefix therefore does not
    narrow the work; the title index does.

    WHY md5(title), not title. A btree on free text can exceed the btree row-size limit
    (about a third of a page) for a long title; md5(title) is always 32 characters, so the
    index cannot refuse a row. The query states `md5(title) = md5(?)` beside the exact
    `title = ?`, so the planner uses the index and the equality still decides the match (an md5
    collision cannot widen it). md5(text) is IMMUTABLE, so it is a valid index expression.

    PARTIAL, WHERE deleted_at IS NULL: the read carries the same tombstone filter exactly, so
    a tombstoned row is never a hit and stays out of the index. Leading column tenant_id, the
    convention of catalog-001's and catalog-041's indexes (every RLS-scoped query filters by
    tenant first). NOT unique: titles repeat across owners and collections.

    BUILD-WINDOW DECISION: plain CREATE INDEX, not CONCURRENTLY, the precedent of
    catalog-041-file-path-batch-index.xml (same table, same reasoning). A plain build takes a
    SHARE lock, which blocks writes and not reads, for the length of the build; the measured
    build is about a third of a second at 250,000 rows, and Liquibase runs the whole changelog
    in the boot-time migration window before the engine serves traffic. The production row
    count is confirmed at deploy time by the Liquibase walk rehearsal against a PITR fork,
    as for every changeset-carrying engine tag. If a store is ever large enough that this stops
    being negligible, build it manually as CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY outside Liquibase.

    DATA EFFECT: none (CREATE INDEX only; no row is read, written, or otherwise touched). A
    non-unique index needs no BYPASSRLS to complete (it validates nothing across tenants).

    Placed after tuples-009-claimed-at.xml and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways
    block, the established late-append convention (D0.1).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="catalog-045-1" author="nexus-gacz0">
        <comment>Partial index on catalog_documents(tenant_id, md5(title)) for live rows, accelerating the exact-title lookup (GET /v1/catalog/by_title). DATA EFFECT: none -- CREATE INDEX only.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true">
CREATE INDEX idx_catalog_documents_title_md5_live
    ON nexus.catalog_documents (tenant_id, md5(title))
 WHERE deleted_at IS NULL;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS nexus.idx_catalog_documents_title_md5_live;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
